<section class="chapter" id="problem">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">1. Problem &amp; Purpose</h2>

      <h3>1.1 The Problem</h3>
      <p>Most organizations believe they are “listening,” but rely on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Low-response engagement surveys</li>
        <li>Anonymous comments with no ownership</li>
        <li>One-off feedback that never loops back</li>
        <li>Sentiment dashboards divorced from decisions</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The result is signal loss, trust erosion, and leadership blind spots.</p>

      <h3>1.2 Purpose</h3>
      <p>
        Listening A[i]gent creates a structured signal layer from inputs already present in day-to-day work —
        preserving attribution and context so teams can act.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Principle:</strong> Listening only “counts” when feedback becomes action, and action becomes visible.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="scope">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">2. Scope &amp; Design Principles</h2>

      <h3>2.1 In-Scope</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Structured capture of qualitative feedback from existing workflows</li>
        <li>Themes, risks, friction points, and “what changed” timelines</li>
        <li>Decision-ready summaries for Ops/HR leadership</li>
        <li>Closed-loop tracking: <em>captured → triaged → actioned → communicated</em></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2.2 Out-of-Scope (v1)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Fully automated sentiment scoring without context</li>
        <li>Anonymous leaderboards or performance management scoring</li>
        <li>Open-ended surveillance (e.g., always-on chat monitoring)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2.3 Design Principles</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Non-invasive.</strong> Use existing artifacts; avoid “extra asks” that drive fatigue.</li>
        <li><strong>Human-in-the-loop.</strong> Humans confirm meaning; AI helps structure and summarize.</li>
        <li><strong>Attribution preserved.</strong> Anonymous inputs only when necessary and explicitly justified.</li>
        <li><strong>Action linkage.</strong> Every insight must map to an owner, a decision, or a next step.</li>
        <li><strong>Auditable.</strong> Preserve sources, dates, and decision notes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2.4 Guardrails</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Clear separation between <strong>signal</strong>, <strong>hypothesis</strong>, and <strong>conclusion</strong>.</li>
        <li>No “AI scoring” used for employee performance decisions.</li>
        <li>Minimum aggregation thresholds before org-level trend reporting.</li>
        <li>Access controls: sensitive signals visible only to appropriate roles.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

